    model_pipeline,
    signature=None,
):

and:

mlflow.transformers.log_model(
    transformers_model=model_pipeline,
    artifact_path=model_name,
    signature=signature,
)
What this test now proves

This is much closer to the integration test you described:

1. Real summarization
Uses your run_pipeline() — no fake model.

2. Real SQLite
Prediction is physically inserted and read from a SQLite DB.

3. Real MLflow server
The test starts an MLflow server whose tracking backend is SQLite.

4. Real MLflow model logging
Uses your log_transformer_model().

5. Real model loading
Uses:

mlflow.pyfunc.load_model(model_uri)

6. Schema
An MLflow signature is attached and the returned prediction is checked against the expected schema.

One thing I would not put into this test yet is model registration/promotion. Your production pipeline does those separately after model logging and validation.

So this should remain a focused “prediction → SQLite → MLflow model → schema” integration test.

